// Movements: countries without land (Movements.cs, MovementRules.cs).
//
// A movement is a country in every way the game knows - a tag, a treasury, relations,
// spirits, decisions, a player who can be it - except that it holds no ground. It has a
// headquarters instead, a point on the map in somebody else's country that it can move; a
// following among the peoples it speaks for, region by region; cadres; standing (legitimacy);
// and a host whose patience it spends. It lives off donations and sponsors. Where its support
// runs high enough it can rise, and an uprising that succeeds makes it a country like any
// other - a capital where the headquarters was, militia out of its cadres, and a war with the
// country it rose against. It does not win a postcode: the whole homeland that the same
// country still governs comes over in the same act, and an agreed vote frees every region
// whose own residents voted for it. One that loses all its ground goes back to being landless.
//
//   tag            the three letters its country goes by
//   peoples        cultures it speaks for: support, donations and cadres come from them
//   faiths         religions whose believers lend it a little support even when not its peoples
//   headquarters   the region its headquarters stands in on the first day
//   homeland       regions it claims: the zone a rising, a carried vote or a return takes
//                  (the AI rises there first)
//   support        support among its peoples, by region, on the first day (0 to 1)
//   sponsors       countries that fund it, in billions a year, while they think well of it
//   recognised_by  countries that recognise it on the first day
//   treasury       billions; cadres people; legitimacy 0 to 1
//   government / ideology   what it is while it has them; state_name what it calls its country
//   territorial_faction    optional history/factions id of the SAME actor; no duplicate HQ
//                          while that administration owns land (not a patron or an ally)
//   traits         rules of its own:
//     armed_wing           uprisings more likely to succeed and raise more militia; campaigns heat the host more
//     tunnel_network       a failed uprising costs fewer cadres; its militia start dug in
//     charity_network      "Social services": support twice as fast as a campaign, and the region lives better
//     diaspora_lobby       donations 60 % higher; "Lobby the host" cools the host's temper
//     street_patrols       "Street patrols": support among its peoples in the host country, and heat
//     hospitallers         "Field hospital": a region lives better and the order's standing rises
//     recognised_sovereign its standing never falls below 0.6
//     government_in_exile  every recognition counts double; "Appeal to the world"
//     humanitarian         its AI never takes up arms: no uprisings, no fighters sent in (a player still may)
//     nonviolence          uprisings are mass protests: carried by standing, cheap to lose, one militia at most
//     refugee_camps        cadres recruit twice as fast
//     for_hire             "Take a contract" for money; recruits at half price; no territorial uprisings.
//                          The base game ships no company: mods/_templates/private_military_company is one
//     territorial_ambition explicitly allows a for_hire company to attempt territorial uprisings (warlord/alternate-history mods)
//
// Later files win, key by key: a mod adds a movement with a key of its own and changes one
// by naming it again with only the fields it changes.
